<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Khambhat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Cambay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Khambhat</a> is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> peninsula from the mainland. It has some of the highest tides in the world. Its ports, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a>, Cambay (Khambhat) and Bhavnagar, were centres of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> for many centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat.jpg" alt="Gulf of Khambhat" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gujarat_Gulfs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
